.TH WIFI_GEN.SH 1 "2026-05-09" "wifi_gen.sh 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wifi_gen.sh \- Generate a Wi-Fi connection QR code from current network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wifi_gen.sh
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B wifi_gen.sh
.RB [ \-\-ssid
.IR SSID ]
.RB [ \-\-password
.IR PASS\ |\ \- ]
.RB [ \-\-security
.IR TYPE ]
.RB [ \-\-hidden
.IR true|false ]
.RB [ \-\-print ]
.RB [ \-\-qr ]
.RB [ \-\-copy ]
.RB [ \-\-list\-networks ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-h\ |\ \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wifi_gen.sh
auto-detects the currently associated Wi-Fi network on the host
system, gathers its SSID, security type, hidden flag, and password,
and produces a Wi-Fi QR code that other devices can scan to join the
network. By default the script opens the rendered QR page in the
system browser; alternative output modes include printing the URL,
rendering an ASCII / UTF-8 QR directly in the terminal, and copying
the URL to the clipboard.
.PP
The QR encoding uses the standard Wi-Fi QR scheme as understood by
iOS, Android, and most modern camera apps. The rendered page is
hosted at
.UR https://dandanilyuk.github.io/wifi_qr_generator/
https://dandanilyuk.github.io/wifi_qr_generator/
.UE .
.PP
Supported platforms:
.TP
.B macOS
Detection uses
.BR networksetup (8),
.BR ipconfig (8),
.BR system_profiler (8),
and the login Keychain via
.BR security (1).
On recent versions of macOS (Sonoma, Sequoia, Tahoe) the SSID may
be redacted by default; the script will prompt for
.BR sudo (8)
to temporarily run
.B ipconfig setverbose 1
in order to reveal it.
.TP
.B Linux
Detection uses NetworkManager via
.BR nmcli (1).
The active Wi-Fi connection's SSID, key-management type, and PSK
are read directly from the connection profile.
.TP
.B Windows / WSL
Detection uses
.BR powershell.exe (1)
and
.BR netsh (1)
("netsh wlan show interfaces" / "netsh wlan show profile
name=<SSID> key=clear"). Both native Git Bash / MSYS / Cygwin and
WSL (which routes through the Windows host) are supported.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-ssid \  SSID
Use the supplied SSID instead of auto-detecting it. Useful when the
host is offline, when the active network is hidden, or when
generating a QR for a network other than the one currently joined.
.TP
.BI \-\-password \  PASS
Use the supplied pre-shared key. If the value is the single
character
.BR \- ,
the password is read from standard input (one line). This avoids
leaking the password through the process listing. See
.B SECURITY
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-pass \  PASS
Alias for
.BR \-\-password .
.TP
.BI \-\-security \  TYPE
Force the security type. Accepted values:
.BR WPA3 ,
.BR WPA2 ,
.BR WPA ,
.BR WEP ,
.BR nopass .
Synonyms for
.B nopass
include
.B none
and
.BR open .
Enterprise / 802.1X variants are normalized to
.B WPA2
with a warning; see
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.TP
.BI \-\-hidden \  true|false
Mark the network as hidden in the resulting QR. When auto-detecting
on macOS, the script will read the hidden flag from
.BR wdutil (8)
if available and if
.BR sudo (8)
can be acquired non-interactively. Otherwise the default is
.BR false .
.TP
.B \-\-print
Print the generated QR URL to standard output instead of opening it
in a browser. Useful for piping into another tool or for headless /
SSH sessions.
.TP
.B \-\-qr
Render the QR code as an ASCII / UTF-8 block directly in the
terminal. Requires
.BR qrencode (1)
to be installed. The terminal QR contains the same Wi-Fi payload as
the URL form and can be scanned directly off the screen by another
device.
.TP
.B \-\-copy
Copy the generated URL to the system clipboard. Uses
.BR pbcopy (1)
on macOS,
.BR wl-copy (1)
or
.BR xclip (1)
or
.BR xsel (1)
on Linux, and
.BR clip.exe
on Windows / WSL.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-networks
List the saved Wi-Fi networks known to the host (Keychain entries on
macOS, NetworkManager connections on Linux, netsh profiles on
Windows) and exit. No QR is generated. Combine with
.BR \-\-ssid
to generate a QR for any saved network without joining it first.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the script version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WIFI_PASSWORD
If set and non-empty, this value is used as the network password and
overrides any auto-detected value. It takes precedence over Keychain
/ NetworkManager / netsh lookups, but is itself overridden by an
explicit
.BR \-\-password
flag. This is the recommended way to supply the password
non-interactively without exposing it in the process listing.
.TP
.B SUDO_ASKPASS
On macOS, when the SSID is redacted and
.B wifi_gen.sh
needs to run
.B ipconfig setverbose 1
as root, it invokes
.BR sudo (8).
The standard
.B SUDO_ASKPASS
variable is honored by sudo for graphical password prompts. The
verbose flag is reset to its previous value on script exit via a
trap.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Auto-detect the current network and open the QR page in the default browser:
.EX
$ wifi_gen.sh
.EE
.TP
Auto-detect the current network and print the QR URL to standard output:
.EX
$ wifi_gen.sh --print
.EE
.TP
Generate a QR for a specific network without joining it:
.EX
$ wifi_gen.sh --ssid "GuestNet" --password "hunter2" --security WPA2
.EE
.TP
Render the QR directly in the terminal (requires
.BR qrencode (1)):
.EX
$ wifi_gen.sh --qr
.EE
.TP
Copy the QR URL to the clipboard for pasting into a chat or document:
.EX
$ wifi_gen.sh --copy
.EE
.TP
List saved networks, then generate a QR for one of them:
.EX
$ wifi_gen.sh --list-networks
$ wifi_gen.sh --ssid "HomeNet" --print
.EE
.TP
Pass the password securely via environment variable:
.EX
$ WIFI_PASSWORD='hunter2' wifi_gen.sh --ssid "GuestNet" --print
.EE
.TP
Pass the password securely via standard input:
.EX
$ printf '%s' 'hunter2' | wifi_gen.sh --ssid "GuestNet" --password - --print
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /Library/Keychains/System.keychain
.PD 0
.TP
.I ~/Library/Keychains/login.keychain-db
.PD
On macOS, Wi-Fi pre-shared keys are stored in the system or login
Keychain and are read via
.BR security\ find\-generic\-password (1).
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/system\-connections/*.nmconnection
On Linux with NetworkManager, saved Wi-Fi profiles (including PSKs)
live in this directory. They are typically root-readable only and
are accessed indirectly via
.BR nmcli (1)
with the
.B \-s
("show secrets") flag.
.TP
.I %ProgramData%\\Microsoft\\Wlansvc\\Profiles\\Interfaces\\
On Windows, WLAN profiles are stored under this path and accessed
via
.BR netsh (1)
("netsh wlan show profile name=<SSID> key=clear").
.SH SECURITY
.B wifi_gen.sh
handles a Wi-Fi pre-shared key in plain text. The following
precautions apply:
.PP
.B Avoid \-\-password VALUE on the command line.
On most Unix systems the full argument list of every running
process is visible to other users via
.BR ps (1)
or
.IR /proc/<pid>/cmdline .
Using
.B \-\-password 'mysecret'
therefore leaks the password to anyone with a shell on the host
while the script is running. Prefer one of the following instead:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B WIFI_PASSWORD
environment variable. This is read once at startup and never
appears in the argument list. Prefix the command with the variable
so it is scoped to a single invocation:
.B WIFI_PASSWORD='secret' wifi_gen.sh ...
.IP \[bu] 2
.BR \-\-password\ \-
to read the password from standard input. Pipe it in with
.BR printf (1)
or a password manager:
.B printf '%s' "$(pass show wifi/home)" | wifi_gen.sh --password -
.PP
.B Generated URLs contain the password.
The QR URL produced by
.B \-\-print
and
.B \-\-copy
encodes the password as a query-string parameter. Treat the URL
itself as a secret: do not paste it into shared chat channels,
public pastebins, browser history that syncs to the cloud, or
shell history files. The hosted page renders the QR locally in the
browser; the password is never sent to a server, but the URL is
still sensitive in transit (anyone with the URL can render the
same QR).
.PP
.B Keychain prompts.
On macOS, reading a Wi-Fi password from the login Keychain via
.BR security (1)
will trigger the standard "Always Allow" / "Allow" dialog the
first time. Choose carefully; "Always Allow" gives the script (and
anyone who can run it) ongoing access to that Keychain item.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.TP
.B Enterprise / 802.1X
WPA-Enterprise (EAP, PEAP, EAP-TLS, etc.) networks cannot be
joined from a Wi-Fi QR code. The Wi-Fi QR specification only
encodes the personal-mode credentials (SSID, PSK, security type,
hidden flag). When
.B wifi_gen.sh
detects an enterprise network it emits a warning, normalizes the
security to
.BR WPA2 ,
and generates a QR anyway; the scanning device will most likely
fail to associate and need to be configured manually with the
enterprise profile.
.TP
.B Hidden-network heuristics on macOS
The hidden-network flag is read from
.BR wdutil (8)
only when sudo is available non-interactively. Otherwise it
defaults to
.BR false .
Pass
.B \-\-hidden true
explicitly if the target network is hidden.
.TP
.B Headless Linux without NetworkManager
On Linux without
.BR nmcli (1)
(for example, systems using
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
directly,
.BR systemd\-networkd (8),
or
.BR iwd (8)
without compatibility shims), auto-detection is not implemented.
Pass
.BR \-\-ssid ", " \-\-password ", and " \-\-security
explicitly.
.TP
.B SSID character set
The QR encoding handles UTF-8 SSIDs and special characters via URL
encoding. However, some scanner apps mishandle SSIDs containing
the literal characters
.BR \\ ", " ; ", " , ", "
or
.BR " " .
If a scan fails, try the
.B \-\-print
form and inspect the generated URL.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nmcli (1),
.BR netsh (1),
.BR networksetup (8),
.BR security (1),
.BR ipconfig (8),
.BR wdutil (8),
.BR qrencode (1),
.BR pbcopy (1),
.BR xclip (1),
.BR wl-copy (1)
